lazysite-hestia for Debian
==========================

HestiaCP integration for lazysite (SM139 increment 4). This package
REPLACES the hand-run installers/hestia scripts of the tarball era
(install-hestia.sh, lazysite-hestia-deploy.sh,
lazysite-hestia-update-all.sh, the lazysite-app template): install the
debs, apply a shipped template, run one command per domain. The old
scripts remain in the source tree only for existing deployments; see
installers/hestia/INSTALL-RUNBOOK.md in the lazysite source for the
full runbook.

The integration is a HOOK-SHAPED COMMAND (lazysite-hestia-domain), not
a Hestia "quick install app": the command shape needs nothing from
Hestia's app framework, works from scripts and cron, and proves the
provisioning model. A panel-native app wrapping the same command is a
possible future addition.

One-off host setup
------------------

1. Install lazysite-common and lazysite-hestia (this package).

2. Copy the web templates into Hestia. Hestia scans templates per
   configured backend - for the standard apache2 + php-fpm setup that
   is /usr/local/hestia/data/templates/web/apache2/php-fpm/ (one level
   deeper than .../apache2/; a template placed too high never appears
   in v-list-web-templates):

       cp /usr/share/lazysite-hestia/templates/lazysite-cgi.* \
          /usr/share/lazysite-hestia/templates/lazysite-fcgi.* \
          /usr/local/hestia/data/templates/web/apache2/php-fpm/

3. Copy the nginx PROXY template into Hestia's OTHER template
   directory. This is a different layer, not a variant of step 2:

       cp /usr/share/lazysite-hestia/templates/lazysite-proxy.* \
          /usr/local/hestia/data/templates/web/nginx/

   Why it matters (SM283): on Hestia the request path is nginx ->
   Apache. lazysite's access rules live in the Apache templates, but
   Hestia's stock nginx proxy serves a fixed list of static EXTENSIONS
   straight off the docroot - so requests for .png, .pdf, .txt and the
   rest are answered before Apache is involved and no ACL can apply.
   A protected section then gates its pages and publishes its images,
   PDFs and archives, silently. The lazysite-proxy template hands
   those requests back to Apache whenever the site has an ACL store,
   and leaves a site with no ACLs serving statics directly at full
   speed as before.

4. Enable the Apache modules the templates use, then restart Apache:

       a2enmod headers rewrite          # both patterns
       a2enmod proxy proxy_fcgi         # lazysite-fcgi only
       a2enmod include                  # only for overlaid SSI (.shtml) sites
       systemctl restart apache2

Per-domain onboarding (3 steps)
-------------------------------

1. Create the web domain in Hestia as usual (panel, or
   `v-add-web-domain <panel-user> <domain>`).

2. Provision it (as root):

       lazysite-hestia-domain add <panel-user> <domain>            # plain CGI
       lazysite-hestia-domain add <panel-user> <domain> --fcgi     # FastCGI pool

   The command prepares the Hestia-specific layout as root (the
   0551-locked domain root, docroot handed to <user>:www-data with
   setgid dirs), then DROPS to the panel user for the actual
   provisioning (`sudo -u <user> lazysite provision ...`) - no root
   writes into the site tree, ownership correct by construction. It
   registers the site in /etc/lazysite/sites.d/ for fleet tooling
   (`lazysite sites`, `lazysite upgrade --all`). `--channel` /
   `--policy` pass through to provisioning.

   With --fcgi it also writes /etc/lazysite/pools/<domain>.conf and
   runs `systemctl enable --now lazysite@<domain>`: a persistent
   FastCGI pool on /run/lazysite/<domain>.sock (~150x faster on cache
   hits; see the lazysite-common README.Debian for the pool pattern).

3. Apply the matching web template (this rebuilds the vhost):

       v-change-web-domain-tpl <panel-user> <domain> lazysite-cgi  yes
   or  v-change-web-domain-tpl <panel-user> <domain> lazysite-fcgi yes

4. Apply the proxy template, so the front end respects the same access
   rules (SM283 - see one-off step 3):

       v-change-web-domain-proxy-tpl <panel-user> <domain> lazysite-proxy

   Confirm which front end answered, without credentials:

       curl -sI https://<domain>/ | grep -i x-lazysite-front
       # X-Lazysite-Front: hestia-proxy/acl

   No header means the domain is still on a stock proxy template and
   any protected section's static files are public. `lazysite-hestia-
   list.sh` flags such domains across the whole host, and
   `lazysite-hestia-update-all.sh --proxy` moves all of them at once.

First-run site steps (manager password via
`sudo -u <user> lazysite users --docroot <docroot> setup-manager`,
optional `webdav_enabled: yes`) are unchanged - see the runbook.

Switching patterns / removing a domain
--------------------------------------

CGI -> FCGI later: `lazysite-hestia-domain add <user> <domain> --fcgi`
is idempotent for an already-provisioned domain (it re-runs provision,
which is a no-op at the same version, and adds the pool), then apply
the lazysite-fcgi template.

`lazysite-hestia-domain remove <domain>` stops and disables the pool
and deregisters the site. It NEVER deletes the docroot; to take the
domain off lazysite, also switch its web template back
(`v-change-web-domain-tpl <user> <domain> default`).

The persistent runtime, on a host with many sites
-------------------------------------------------

SM666 ships a per-instance runtime that holds work the request path
cannot - a scheduler now, transports later. It is templated the same
way as the FastCGI pool:

    /etc/lazysite/daemon/<domain>.conf     DOCROOT= and USER=
    systemctl enable --now lazysited@<domain>

THERE ARE TWO SWITCHES AND BOTH MUST BE ON. This unit, enabled by you;
and the `daemon` plugin, enabled by the site's sysop on the Plugin
Manager page. The plugin is born disabled (ADR 0009), and the manager
has no root, so enabling it cannot start a service. That is deliberate
rather than an oversight, and the runtime reports it honestly:

    lazysited --docroot <docroot> --status

answers `desired: up` with `runtime: not-started` when the plugin is on
and the unit is not, so you can see which switch is missing.

WHAT IT COSTS ON A HOST WITH HUNDREDS OF SITES: nothing, until somebody
turns it on. An uninstantiated unit does not exist. An instantiated one
whose plugin is disabled exits immediately, without creating so much as
a state directory - and the unit uses `Restart=on-failure`, not
`always`, precisely so that exit is respected rather than restarted in
a loop. What runs is the number of sites that deliberately enabled it.

The runtime never runs as root. The unit starts it as root only because
systemd cannot template `User=` from an instance name and the instance
name is a domain; lazysited drops to USER before loading any daemon
code. Started by hand it must be run as that user, or as root with
--user; it refuses to run the site's scheduled jobs as root.

A scheduled job additionally runs as a lazysite ACCOUNT - set
`daemon_job_user` in the site's daemon config, and give that account
`run_jobs`. A purpose-made account is preferred to a person's: a
person's rotates, changes and eventually leaves. No account, or an
account without `run_jobs`, means no job runs, and the refusal is
recorded with its reason.


Upgrades
--------

Upgrading these packages never touches site trees. To roll sites
forward from the new payload:

    lazysite upgrade --all            # respects per-site channel/policy
    lazysite upgrade --all --force-security   # security-critical releases

